{
  "notes": "<h3>Basics</h3><ul><li>$i = \\sqrt{-1}$, $i^2=-1$, $i^3=-i$, $i^4=1$; powers of $i$ cycle with period 4. $i^{4k}=1$.</li><li>A complex number $z = a+ib$ has real part $\\text{Re}(z)=a$, imaginary part $\\text{Im}(z)=b$.</li></ul><h3>Algebra of complex numbers</h3><ul><li>$(a+ib)+(c+id) = (a+c)+i(b+d)$; $(a+ib)(c+id) = (ac-bd)+i(ad+bc)$.</li><li>Conjugate $\\bar z = a-ib$; $z\\bar z = a^2+b^2 = |z|^2$.</li><li>Division: multiply numerator & denominator by conjugate of denominator.</li><li>Modulus $|z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$; Argument $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(b/a)$ (adjusted for quadrant).</li></ul><h3>Polar / Euler form</h3><ul><li>$z = r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta) = re^{i\\theta}$, $r=|z|$.</li><li>$|z_1z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$; $\\arg(z_1z_2) = \\arg z_1+\\arg z_2$.</li><li>$|z_1+z_2| \\le |z_1|+|z_2|$ (triangle inequality).</li></ul><table><tr><th>Property</th><th>Formula</th></tr><tr><td>$\\overline{z_1+z_2}$</td><td>$\\bar z_1+\\bar z_2$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\overline{z_1z_2}$</td><td>$\\bar z_1\\bar z_2$</td></tr><tr><td>$z^{-1}$</td><td>$\\bar z/|z|^2$</td></tr></table><h3>Quadratic Equations</h3><ul><li>For $ax^2+bx+c=0$ ($a\\ne0$): roots $x = \\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{D}}{2a}$, $D=b^2-4ac$ is the discriminant.</li><li>$D>0$: real, distinct roots (perfect square $\\Rightarrow$ rational); $D=0$: real, equal roots; $D<0$: complex conjugate roots $\\dfrac{-b\\pm i\\sqrt{|D|}}{2a}$.</li><li>Sum of roots $\\alpha+\\beta = -b/a$; product $\\alpha\\beta = c/a$.</li><li>Equation with given roots: $x^2-(\\alpha+\\beta)x+\\alpha\\beta = 0$.</li></ul><h3>Exam tip</h3><p>KCET typically asks 2 questions from this chapter — one on complex number algebra/modulus-argument, one on quadratic roots (nature of roots or forming an equation from roots). Practice rationalising complex fractions and computing $D$ quickly; sign errors in $D=b^2-4ac$ are the most common mistake.</p>",
